{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/checker/checker.go",
      "symbol": "resolveCallExpression",
      "line": 8645,
      "explanation": "Entry point for an ordinary call. It checks the callee expression (line 8670), strips optional-chain wrappers, computes the callee's apparent type, and obtains the callable signatures with getSignaturesOfType(apparentType, SignatureKindCall) (line 8698) while also counting construct signatures. It handles super/import/untyped calls and the untyped-function-call case (line 8703), then for a typed function call delegates to resolveCall(node, callSignatures, candidatesOutArray, checkMode, callChainFlags, nil) at line 8746. It also defers generic functions whose signature returns a function when CheckModeSkipGenericFunctions is set (line 8742), returning resolvingSignature so outer inference can accumulate. Thus it is the function that turns the callee's type into the candidate signature list and hands the typed call to the overload resolver."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/checker/checker.go",
      "symbol": "resolveCall",
      "line": 9028,
      "explanation": "Overload-resolution coordinator. It builds a CallState (node, type arguments, effective arguments) and orders the candidate overloads via reorderCandidates (line 9043), which moves literal-typed/specialized signatures ahead of generic ones and applies optional-call-chain signatures. It decides whether context-sensitive arguments must be excluded on the first pass: when there is more than one candidate (or a single generic candidate) and some argument is context sensitive, it sets s.argCheckMode = CheckModeSkipContextSensitive (lines 9067-9072). It then runs the successive type-relation passes: chooseOverload with the subtypeRelation when there is more than one candidate (line 9111), and, if that yields nothing, chooseOverload with the assignableRelation (line 9114). If both fail it produces a fallback the getCandidateForOverloadFailure way (longest candidate or union of overloads, line 9120), pre-caches it as the resolved signature (line 9128), and, when errors are reportable, calls reportCallResolutionErrors (line 9140) using the error candidates collected in the CallState."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/checker/checker.go",
      "symbol": "chooseOverload",
      "line": 9213,
      "explanation": "Evaluates individual candidates for one relation pass. It first clears the error-candidate slots (candidatesForArgumentError, candidateForArgumentArityError, candidateForTypeArgumentError). For a single non-generic candidate it checks type-argument presence and hasCorrectArity and then isSignatureApplicable (lines 9217-9226). Otherwise it loops over candidates, skipping those failing hasCorrectTypeArgumentArity or hasCorrectArity (line 9229). For generic candidates it either validates explicitly supplied type arguments with checkTypeArguments (line 9237) or infers them with inferTypeArguments (line 9248) using s.argCheckMode, then instantiates via getSignatureInstantiation and re-checks arity when the signature has a generic rest type (lines 9257-9263). Applicability of the (instantiated) candidate is tested with isSignatureApplicable at the current s.argCheckMode (line 9267). Crucially, if the candidate is applicable and s.argCheckMode is still non-zero (context-sensitive arguments had been excluded), it switches s.argCheckMode to CheckModeNormal and re-runs inferTypeArguments and isSignatureApplicable for the same candidate (lines 9272-9291) so the callback arguments are now contextually typed, before returning the candidate (line 9293). Failures are recorded and it returns nil if no candidate passes."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "For an ordinary call, resolveCallExpression checks the callee, extracts call signatures from its apparent type, and delegates the typed call to resolveCall. resolveCall orders candidates with reorderCandidates, captures the argument list, and, when a callback (a context-sensitive function expression) is present among multiple candidates, sets argCheckMode = CheckModeSkipContextSensitive. It then invokes chooseOverload twice: first with the subtypeRelation pass (only when there is more than one candidate), then with the assignableRelation pass. chooseOverload walks the ordered candidates, enforcing type-argument arity and call arity, validating explicit type arguments or inferring type parameters, instantiating the signature, and testing applicability with isSignatureApplicable. The first time a candidate is otherwise applicable, the callback has not yet been contextually typed, so inference cannot use its parameter types. That is why the same candidate is checked a second time: chooseOverload sees the non-zero argCheckMode, flips it to CheckModeNormal, re-infers type arguments and re-checks applicability (isSignatureApplicable/checkExpressionWithContextualType) so the callback is now typed against the selected candidate's parameter, preventing premature/permanent contextual typing from biasing overload selection. If a candidate succeeds, it is returned through resolveCall. If no candidate succeeds in either relation pass, chooseOverload returns nil for both; resolveCall then calls getCandidateForOverloadFailure, which either picks the longest candidate (inferring type args with CheckModeSkipContextSensitive) or, for multiple non-generic overloads and no candidatesOutArray, synthesizes a union signature combining the overloads. This fallback is cached as the resolved signature so the call still has a type, and if error reporting is enabled resolveCall calls reportCallResolutionErrors, which reports in priority order: the last argument-error candidate (with 'No overload matches this call' and 'The last overload gave the following error' chains), otherwise an argument arity error, otherwise a type-argument error, otherwise a general arity/type-argument-arity error."
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/project/projectcollectionbuilder.go",
      "symbol": "refreshContentMapperProjectForChanges",
      "line": 465,
      "explanation": "Role (1): the project-collection helper that decides whether watched paths or a refresh-all request affect a mapper project, refreshes it, and strips single-file-update eligibility. It begins with the early exit `if project.Program == nil || project.contentMapperWatchedFiles == nil { return }`, so a project with no built program or no watcher set is skipped even for refresh-all. It then sets `affected := refreshAll`; when false it tests `slices.ContainsFunc(paths, project.contentMapperWatchedFiles.Has)`. If not affected it returns. Otherwise it calls `project.Program.ContentMapperProject().Refresh()` and then `entry.Change` sets `project.dirty = true` and `project.dirtyFilePath = ""`, which removes single-file-update eligibility (project.go:524 only takes the UpdateProgram single-file path when dirtyFilePath != \"\"), forcing a full program rebuild. It is invoked for every project at line 404 with `refreshAll = summary.HasExcessiveNonCreateWatchEvents()` (InvalidateAll or >1000 changed+deleted events; filechange.go:74-75). Notably the Refresh error is discarded (`_ = ...Refresh()`), so invalidation is not stopped by a close error."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/contentmapper/hostimpl.go",
      "symbol": "Refresh",
      "line": 877,
      "explanation": "Role (2): the mapper project lease's refresh routine. It takes `lifecycleMu.RLock()` then `host.mu.Lock()` and returns early if `host.projects == nil` (host closed). For each entry in the lease it skips unopened entries, and for opened entries with a live connection it calls `host.closeProject(...)` and accumulates the error with `errors.Join`; crucially it then unconditionally sets `entry.opened = false`. So a failed remote close does NOT leave the local entry opened: the local state is marked closed so the next lazy `openProjectLocked` reopens it. It returns the joined close error, but the project helper at projectcollectionbuilder.go:480 ignores it and still marks the project dirty, so invalidation continues even on a failed close."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/contentmapper/hostimpl.go",
      "symbol": "openProjectLocked",
      "line": 650,
      "explanation": "Role (3): the host routine that lazily opens a remote project, validates the dynamic/static identity and watched-file contracts, and commits successful metadata. It returns nil if `entry.opened`; otherwise `connForLocked` lazily dials/spawns the mapper process. It marshals compiler options and issues `MethodOpenProject` with ConfigFileName, ProjectHandle, mapper Options, and CompilerOptions. It enforces: dynamic config requires a non-empty ConfigIdentity (ProjectErrorKindMissingConfigIdentity), static config forbids a ConfigIdentity (ProjectErrorKindUnexpectedConfigIdentity) and forbids WatchedFiles (ProjectErrorKindUnexpectedWatchedFiles), and every watched file must satisfy `tspath.PathIsAbsolute` (ProjectErrorKindNonAbsoluteWatchedFile). Only after validation does it commit `entry.configIdentity`, `entry.watchedFiles`, `entry.optionDiagnostics`, and finally `entry.opened = true`, so a rejected open is retried on the next call."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/project/compilerhost.go",
      "symbol": "GetContentMappedSourceFiles",
      "line": 113,
      "explanation": "Role (4): the editor compiler-host routine that gets a mapped source-file bundle through the identity- and locale-sensitive cache, binds a newly produced bundle, and checks supplemental filename collisions. It fetches the file handle, computes `identity, err := project.Identity(mapper)`, hashes it (`xxh3.Hash128`), and derives the cache key with `contentMappedParseCacheKey(parseOptions, fh.Hash(), transformIdentity, locale.FromContext(c.builder.ctx))`, folding original-content hash, transform identity, and diagnostic locale into one hash (parsecache.go:44-52). It calls `contentMappedParseCache.AcquireOrError`; the produce closure runs `contentmapper.TransformAndParse`, stamps `files.Canonical.Hash = key.Hash`, binds the canonical and every supplemental `SourceFile`, and returns the whole `SourceFiles` bundle. After a successful acquire (including a cache hit) it always runs `contentmapper.CheckSupplementalFileNameCollisions(files, c.FS().FileExists)`, and on collision calls `c.builder.contentMappedParseCache.Deref(key)` to undo the acquisition before returning the error."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/project/refcountcache.go",
      "symbol": "AcquireOrError",
      "line": 86,
      "explanation": "Role (5): the generic reference-counted acquisition routine. It calls `loadOrStoreNewLockedEntry(identity)`, which returns a locked entry with refcount incremented (initialized to 1 when new). If loaded, it returns the stored value. Otherwise it runs the caller's `produce()` while holding the new entry's lock, so concurrent misses for the same key serialize on that lock. On `produce` error it sets `entry.refCount = 0`, deletes the speculative entry from the map, and returns the error, so failures are not cached. On success it stores the value. A concurrent waiter that observed the entry blocks on `entry.mu`; after the producer unlocks, the waiter re-checks `existing.refCount <= 0` in `loadOrStoreNewLockedEntry` and, seeing the killed entry, recurses to create a fresh entry and re-produce rather than observing the failed zero value."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "Call/state flow: ProjectCollectionBuilder.DidChangeFiles collects all changed/deleted/created paths and, when a content mapper host exists, calls refreshContentMapperProjectForChanges for every project with `refreshAll = summary.HasExcessiveNonCreateWatchEvents()` (projectcollectionbuilder.go:401-407). That helper (projectcollectionbuilder.go:465) early-exits when project.Program == nil or project.contentMapperWatchedFiles == nil. The watched-file set is populated during CreateProgram from mapper package.json paths plus dynamic watchedFiles reported by ContentMapperProject().WatchedFiles() (projectcollectionbuilder.go:1492-1512), converted to absolute tspath.Path values. A watched-path hit (paths contains a set member) refreshes that project; an unrelated path leaves it untouched (return without Refresh and without dirtying); refresh-all (InvalidateAll or more than excessiveChangeThreshold=1000 changed+deleted events) refreshes every qualifying project. On a hit/refresh-all it calls project.Program.ContentMapperProject().Refresh() then sets dirty=true and dirtyFilePath=\"\", which forbids the single-file UpdateProgram path (project.go:524) and forces a full rebuild so re-parsing picks up mapped changes. If Program is nil or the watched set is nil, nothing happens even for refresh-all. Refresh (hostimpl.go:877) takes lifecycleMu.RLock then host.mu.Lock, closes each opened entry's remote project handle via closeProject, joins errors, and then unconditionally sets entry.opened=false, so a failed remote close does NOT leave the local entry opened; it is marked for reopening. openProjectLocked is called lazily from Project/Identities/Identity/WatchedFiles/Transform and reopens it. The helper discards Refresh's returned error (`_ =`), so invalidation is not stopped on a close failure.\n\nOpen/reopen and identity: openProjectLocked (hostimpl.go:650) lazily dials through connForLocked and calls MethodOpenProject; it validates dynamic-vs-static identity and watched-file contracts before committing metadata: a dynamic mapper (Manifest.DynamicConfig) must return a non-empty configIdentity (MissingConfigIdentity), a static mapper must return neither configIdentity (UnexpectedConfigIdentity) nor watchedFiles (UnexpectedWatchedFiles), and every watched file must be absolute (NonAbsoluteWatchedFile). This is why a static mapper returning a configuration identity or watched files is rejected (it declared no dynamic dependency, so those fields are contract violations), a dynamic mapper returning an empty identity is rejected (its cache identity would be unstable/unusable), and a relative watched filename is rejected (the watched set is matched against absolute project paths via contentMapperWatchedFiles.Has). Project.Identity produces, for dynamic mappers, combinedIdentity(mapper, configIdentity, compilerOptions) which folds the mapper identity, mapper options, configIdentity, and TransformIdentity into one hash (hostimpl.go:636-648, 918, 947); for static mappers it uses mapper identity plus TransformIdentity (hostimpl.go:920-921, 949-950). GetContentMappedSourceFiles (compilerhost.go:113) hashes that identity and builds ContentMappedParseCacheKey from parse options, the original content hash, the transform identity hash, and the diagnostic locale (parsecache.go:44-52). Therefore reopening after Refresh with the SAME configuration identity, same source bytes, and same parse options yields the same key and the cached canonical+supplemental bundle is reused; reopening with a DIFFERENT configuration identity changes combinedIdentity and thus the transform-identity component of the key, so a distinct bundle is produced even though source bytes and parse options are unchanged. Changing only the diagnostic locale changes the locale bytes appended to the key hash (and SetLocale restarts mapper processes), so it also separates the bundle even when the mapper identity/transform identity are unchanged. In short, a bundle is shared only when parse options, raw content hash, transform identity, and diagnostic locale all match; any difference separates them. One refcount entry owns the whole SourceFiles bundle (canonical plus supplementals) and callers ref/deref only the canonical key (parsecache.go:86-95).\n\nCache acquisition: both the ordinary parse cache and the content-mapped cache use RefCountCache.AcquireOrError (refcountcache.go:86). For two concurrent misses on the same key, loadOrStoreNewLockedEntry returns a locked entry to the first caller (refCount initialized to 1) and the second caller blocks on entry.mu. If the first production succeeds, it stores the value, unlocks, and the waiter then increments refCount and returns the same cached value (successful production is shared). If the first production fails, AcquireOrError sets refCount=0, deletes the speculative entry, and returns the error; the waiter, after acquiring the lock, checks refCount <= 0 in loadOrStoreNewLockedEntry, discards the dead entry, and starts a fresh acquisition/production. So the waiter must re-produce and does not observe a cached failed (zero-value) bundle; failures are deliberately never cached. Note DisableDeletion is test-only and the failure path deletes regardless.\n\nTransform error vs supplemental collision: a transformation error is returned by the produce closure, so AcquireOrError itself undoes the acquisition internally (refCount=0 and delete, refcountcache.go:92-98) and GetContentMappedSourceFiles returns without ever holding a reference. A physical-file collision is detected only after production succeeded and the bundle was stored; CheckSupplementalFileNameCollisions (transform.go:128-135) runs outside the produce closure on every call, so on a cache hit, where produce is skipped, it still executes and can reject a bundle whose compiler-assigned .0/.1 virtual filename has since been created on disk. Because a stored bundle now exists and is referenced, the caller must undo the acquisition explicitly with contentMappedParseCache.Deref(key) (compilerhost.go:144-147), which differs from the internal rollback on transform failure. Reference ownership is for the output bundle rather than each supplemental file: supplementals are compiler-assigned, unnamed derivatives identified only by the canonical key plus index, and keeping one refcount on the whole SourceFiles value (parsecache.go:86-89) ensures the canonical and its supplementals are acquired, reused, and released together, avoiding over/under-deref and partial-lifetime bugs (as exercised by TestContentMappedParseCacheBundleLifetime and the duplicate-refcount tests). Supporting evidence: host_test.go TestProjectRejectsRelativeWatchedFiles:1112, TestDynamicProjectRequiresConfigIdentity:1135, and TestStaticMapperRejectsDynamicProjectResponseFields:1159 confirm the openProjectLocked rejections; host_test.go TestRunnerLeaseLifecycle:833 covers lease lifecycle; the content-mapper Project interface documents Refresh as closing opened handles for reopening (host.go:273-284)."
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/checker/checker.go",
      "symbol": "resolveCallExpression",
      "line": 8645,
      "explanation": "Entry point for resolving an ordinary call expression (KindCallExpression). It first type-checks the callee expression, strips optional-chain markers, checks for possibly-null/undefined, and computes the apparent type (c.getApparentType(funcType)). It then obtains the callable signatures that drive overload resolution by calling c.getSignaturesOfType(apparentType, SignatureKindCall) (line 8698) and counts construct signatures (line 8699). If the callee is an untyped function call or has no call signatures it delegates to resolveUntypedCall/resolveErrorCall; otherwise, for a typed call it delegates overload resolution by calling c.resolveCall(node, callSignatures, candidatesOutArray, checkMode, callChainFlags, nil) (line 8746). It also implements the deferred/inner-generic-call heuristic that returns c.resolvingSignature when CheckModeSkipGenericFunctions is set and a generic candidate returns a function type (lines 8742-8745)."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/checker/checker.go",
      "symbol": "resolveCall",
      "line": 9028,
      "explanation": "Orchestrates overload resolution. It builds a CallState (node, type arguments, effective arguments, candidates), reorders the incoming signatures with c.reorderCandidates(signatures, callChainFlags) (line 9043) to prefer literal-type/specialized overloads and maintain declaration grouping, and exposes them through candidatesOutArray for signature help. Crucially it decides the argument check mode: if there is more than one candidate or a generic single candidate and some argument is context sensitive (c.isContextSensitive), it sets s.argCheckMode = CheckModeSkipContextSensitive (lines 9068-9069), otherwise CheckModeNormal. It then performs the successive type-relation passes, calling c.chooseOverload(&s, c.subtypeRelation) when there is more than one candidate and, if that yields nothing, c.chooseOverload(&s, c.assignableRelation) (lines 9110-9115). If both passes fail it computes a fallback signature via c.getCandidateForOverloadFailure, caches it as the resolved signature to break circularity, and reports diagnostics via c.reportCallResolutionErrors (lines 9120-9141), which prefers the last argument-error candidate, then an arity error candidate, then a type-argument error candidate, and finally generic argument/type-argument arity errors."
    },
    {
      "path": "tsc/internal/checker/checker.go",
      "symbol": "chooseOverload",
      "line": 9213,
      "explanation": "Evaluates individual candidates for one relation (subtype or assignable). It resets per-pass error trackers (candidatesForArgumentError, candidateForArgumentArityError, candidateForTypeArgumentError), then iterates s.candidates in order. For each candidate it applies arity checks: c.hasCorrectTypeArgumentArity(candidate, s.typeArguments) and c.hasCorrectArity(...), skipping candidates that fail. For generic candidates it either validates explicitly supplied type arguments with c.checkTypeArguments (recording candidateForTypeArgumentError on failure) or creates an inference context and runs c.inferTypeArguments with s.argCheckMode|CheckModeSkipGenericFunctions, then instantiates the signature. It checks applicability with c.isSignatureApplicable(..., s.argCheckMode, false, nil), which contextually types arguments via c.checkExpressionWithContextualType and records the candidate in candidatesForArgumentError on failure. If the candidate satisfies the first pass and s.argCheckMode was non-zero (context-sensitive arguments initially excluded), it flips s.argCheckMode to CheckModeNormal and re-runs inference and applicability for that same candidate so the callback arguments are contextually typed against the now-known overload; on success it writes the instantiated candidate back into s.candidates and returns it. If the loop exhausts without success it returns nil, leaving the best error candidate for resolveCall to report."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "For a typed ordinary call, resolveCallExpression checks the callee, takes its apparent type, extracts call signatures with getSignaturesOfType, and delegates to resolveCall. resolveCall constructs the CallState, reorders candidates via reorderCandidates (specialized/literal signatures first), and determines the argument check mode. When there is more than one candidate (or a single generic candidate) and any argument is context sensitive — e.g. a callback whose parameter type can only be fixed once the overload is known — it sets argCheckMode to CheckModeSkipContextSensitive. It then runs chooseOverload twice: first with the subtype relation (to select the best-matching overload), then, if that returns nothing, with the assignable relation. chooseOverload iterates candidates in order, enforcing type-argument arity and argument arity, validating explicit type arguments or inferring them, and calling isSignatureApplicable; isSignatureApplicable contextually types each argument (via checkExpressionWithContextualType) against the candidate parameter and tests assignability. A candidate may be checked again after initially excluding context-sensitive arguments because of this two-phase design: the first pass deliberately ignores context-sensitive (callback/function) arguments so that inference and overload selection are not confused by contextual typing that depends on the not-yet-chosen overload; once a candidate passes that first pass, chooseOverload flips argCheckMode to CheckModeNormal and repeats inference and applicability for that candidate, now contextually typing the callbacks against the selected overload's parameter types. If no candidate succeeds in either relation pass, resolveCall calls getCandidateForOverloadFailure (choosing the longest candidate, or a combined/union signature when candidates are non-generic) and caches it as the resolved signature, then reportCallResolutionErrors emits the most informative diagnostic: an argument-not-assignable error on the last argument-error candidate if one exists, otherwise an argument-arity error candidate, otherwise a type-argument error candidate, otherwise an overall type-argument or argument-arity error across the signatures that had correct type-argument arity."
}

{"findings":[{"path":"tsc/internal/checker/nodebuilderimpl.go","symbol":"symbolToTypeNode","line":650,"explanation":"Role 1, the symbol-to-type-syntax serializer. It computes the accessible symbol chain with lookupSymbolChain (line 651) and then branches: if chain[0] is a non-global external module, it must emit import-type syntax (comments/line 656-657); otherwise it builds a qualified/entity name and wraps it as TypeReference or TypeQuery (lines 728-742). For the import-type branch it resolves a module specifier, and under Node16/NodeNext it forces an ESM mode override when the target file is ESM but the context file is not (lines 670-676). If the resulting specifier contains '/node_modules/' and the flags forbid node_modules relative paths, it retries getSpecifierForModuleSymbol with a swapped resolution mode and, if that drops the node_modules segment, records importModeOverride (lines 680-697); only if the retry still lies in node_modules does it set encounteredError and call tracker.ReportLikelyUnsafeImportRequiredError (lines 699-704). This is the module-mode/portability fallback that later feeds createImportAttributesForModuleSpecifier (line 707)."},{"path":"tsc/internal/checker/symbolaccessibility.go","symbol":"isSymbolAccessibleWorker","line":843,"explanation":"Role 2, the accessibility worker. It first delegates accessible-chain discovery by calling IsAnySymbolAccessible (line 845), which itself calls getAccessibleSymbolChain (line 35) and hasVisibleDeclarations, returning an accessible result carrying AliasesToMakeVisible. If no result is produced and the symbol's declaration lives in an external module different from the enclosing declaration's module, it returns SymbolAccessibilityCannotBeNamed with ErrorModuleName (lines 850-863) - i.e. an unnameable external-module symbol. Otherwise it returns SymbolAccessibilityNotAccessible with only ErrorSymbolName (lines 866-870) - an inaccessible local name. IsSymbolAccessible (line 839) is the public adapter, and EmitResolver.IsSymbolAccessible (checker/emitresolver.go:685) forwards through this worker without re-locking because the caller already holds the checker lock."},{"path":"tsc/internal/transformers/declarations/tracker.go","symbol":"TrackSymbol","line":189,"explanation":"Role 3, the declaration tracker's symbol-observation entry. It immediately returns false for any symbol with SymbolFlagsTypeParameter (lines 190-192), so type parameters never participate in alias discovery. If the symbol equals the specially watched watchedClassSymbol (set in transform.go:1386 while serializing a class-expression-derived declaration), it merely records classSymbolTracked = true and returns false without reporting an accessibility error (lines 196-199), leaving visibility to be handled by namespace wrapping. For all other symbols it calls the resolver's IsSymbolAccessible with shouldComputeAliasToMarkVisible = true (line 200), the request that asks the checker to compute AliasesToMakeVisible, and passes the result to handleSymbolAccessibilityError."},{"path":"tsc/internal/transformers/declarations/tracker.go","symbol":"handleSymbolAccessibilityError","line":204,"explanation":"Role 4, the accessibility-result consumer. On SymbolAccessibilityAccessible it appends every entry of symbolAccessibilityResult.AliasesToMakeVisible into state.lateMarkedStatements via core.AppendIfUnique (lines 205-211) - the alias worklist that the drain later consumes. On any accessibility value other than NotResolved it resolves the context-dependent diagnostic through s.state.getSymbolAccessibilityDiagnostic (line 217), which the transform sets per declaration kind via setupDiagnosticContext / createGetSymbolAccessibilityDiagnosticForNode, and reports either the typeName or plain form (lines 218-230). For SymbolAccessibilityNotResolved it deliberately falls through and reports nothing (line 215), because the checker already emits the unresolved-name error; emitting a second declaration-emit accessibility diagnostic would be wrong."},{"path":"tsc/internal/transformers/declarations/transform.go","symbol":"transformAndReplaceLatePaintedStatements","line":386,"explanation":"Role 5, the declaration-transform worklist drain. It loops while state.lateMarkedStatements is non-empty (lines 401-404), popping each queued alias (lines 406-407), re-running transformTopLevelDeclaration on it (line 412), then keying the result by the ORIGINAL node identity: original := EmitContext().MostOriginal(next), id := GetNodeId(original), lateStatementReplacementMap[id] = result (lines 415-417). Because handling one alias can mark further aliases visible, the worklist grows during the drain - the loop handles this. Afterwards it rebuilds the output list (lines 422-459): statements that are not IsLateVisibilityPaintedStatement pass through; for painted statements it looks up the map by MostOriginal/id and distinguishes three cases - no map entry means keep the original statement (lines 431-434), a nil replacement means delete the statement (lines 435-437), and a KindSyntaxList replacement means expand/splice all its children into the output (lines 438-449); a single node replacement replaces the statement one-for-one (lines 450-458), while updating needsScopeFixMarker / resultHasExternalModuleIndicator. transformTopLevelDeclaration also filters duplicates of the current input out of the queue (lines 1719-1721)."}

],"flow":"Execution order is not the role numbering. Initial transform begins in visitSourceFile (transform.go:280), which resets the per-file state: lateMarkedStatements = empty (line 293), lateStatementReplacementMap = empty (line 294), and installs throwDiagnostic as the placeholder accessibility-diagnostic selector (line 290). transformSourceFile (line 341) visits statements; visitDeclarationStatements (line 1161) intercepts each top-level declaration, and for the default case stores a placeholder transform in lateStatementReplacementMap keyed by the MostOriginal node id but returns the original node (lines 1182-1188). createGetSymbolAccessibilityDiagnosticForNode/setupDiagnosticContext (transform.go:551, diagnostics.go:139) is the adapter that sets state.getSymbolAccessibilityDiagnostic per node kind, while suppressNewDiagnosticContexts guards nested type-literal/mapped-type and variable contexts (transform.go:553-564, 849).

The real callback chain is: while serializing a declaration's inferred type (ensureType -> resolver.CreateTypeOfDeclaration, transform.go:1691) the NodeBuilder invokes role 1 symbolToTypeNode. symbolToTypeNode calls lookupSymbolChain, which calls role 3 TrackSymbol (nodebuilderimpl.go:1061-1062). TrackSymbol calls resolver.IsSymbolAccessible with shouldComputeAliasToMarkVisible=true (tracker.go:200). EmitResolver.IsSymbolAccessible (emitresolver.go:685) is the adapter that reaches role 2 isSymbolAccessibleWorker, which delegates to IsAnySymbolAccessible -> getAccessibleSymbolChain + hasVisibleDeclarations (emitresolver.go:384-465), which both flips declarationLinks.isVisible and collects AliasesToMakeVisible. The result flows back through role 3 into role 4 handleSymbolAccessibilityError, which either reports the context-dependent diagnostic or appends the newly visible aliasing statements to lateMarkedStatements. Only after the whole initial pass does role 5 transformAndReplaceLatePaintedStatements drain that worklist.

Why draining only the initially present aliases leaves stale output: alias visibility is transitive. The characteristic case is `module A { export module Q {} import B = Q; import C = B; export import D = C; }` (documented at transform.go:386-400). Initially only Q and D are visible. Checking D makes C visible and queues C; transforming C makes B visible and queues B. If the loop were bounded to a snapshot of the worklist, C and B would never be re-transformed; their replacement-map entries would retain the early elided form (isDeclarationAndNotVisible -> transformTopLevelDeclaration returns nil at line 1738-1739), so the emitted declaration would be missing the aliases needed to name the type - stale output. The while-loop plus AppendIfUnique exists precisely to process this growing transitive set.

Replacement semantics in the final rebuild (lines 423-458): if IsLateVisibilityPaintedStatement is false the statement is copied through; if the map has no entry the original statement is kept (no replacement); if the entry is nil the statement is dropped (deletion, e.g. a stripped-internal or now-invisible declaration); if the entry's Kind is SyntaxList its children are appended individually (expansion into several statements, e.g. a require binding pattern lowered to multiple import declarations in transformCjsRequireVariableDeclaration, transform.go:875-920); otherwise exactly one node replaces the statement. Map lookups always use tx.EmitContext().MostOriginal(statement) + GetNodeId so that repeatedly transformed nodes still resolve to the single original statement (lines 415-417 and 428-430).

Unresolved names avoid an extra diagnostic because role 4 explicitly excludes SymbolAccessibilityNotResolved (tracker.go:215); isEntityNameVisible produces that state when the first identifier cannot be resolved at all (emitresolver.go:362-368), and the checker will report the unresolved name itself, so declaration emit must stay silent. Retrying module spelling with a different resolution mode prevents a portability error as follows: when the first specifier dives into /node_modules/, symbolToTypeNode under Node16/NodeNext recomputes the specifier with swappedMode (ESNext or CommonJS opposite the context file) and, if the retry no longer contains /node_modules/, sets importModeOverride to that mode (nodebuilderimpl.go:680-696). Since importModeOverride is then non-None, the ReportLikelyUnsafeImportRequiredError path at lines 699-704 is skipped and the import type is emitted with the mode assertion, keeping the reference portable.

Resulting declaration-emit invariants: (1) any internal import alias reached by an exported declaration's inferred type is made visible and re-emitted, closing the alias chain so the .d.ts is self-contained; (2) each late visibility-painted original statement is emitted exactly once via the original-node-id keyed replacement map, with duplicates filtered from the worklist; (3) no replacement keeps the original, nil deletes, SyntaxList expands, single node replaces; (4) type parameters and watched class-expression self-references are never turned into accessibility errors; (5) unresolved names get no duplicate diagnostic; (6) import types prefer a portable spelling, retrying a mode override before declaring the reference non-portable. Limits shown by this revision: the comment at transform.go:400 states that today dependent alias marking only happens for internal import aliases - external-module/re-export aliases are not transitively painted this way, so those can still yield CannotBeNamed results. The worklist and replacement map are also per-source-file state (reset in visitSourceFile), and the watched-class-symbol mechanism is a narrow special case set only in the class-expression-to-declaration transform (transform.go:1386-1404), not general symbol tracking."}

