ndMappings, which fileloader.go:515-516 localizes as 'The content mapper ... did not provide the required position mappings' and the file becomes a failure stub (emptyContentMappedFile, fileloader.go:587, SpanMap()==nil). The host.go:173-176 and hostimpl.go:1186-1188 comments state the contract explicitly: nil means 'not content-mapped'/no map supplied, empty means fully synthesized output.\n\nExternal diagnostics must not be mapped twice. An external mapper diagnostic is attached to the canonical SourceFile, which does have a SpanMap, but its location is already an original-text range produced by originalPositions.normalize in decodeTransformResult. isSynthesizedContentMappedDiagnostic (diagnostics.go:86) explicitly excludes any diag with Source()!='' so it is never bucketed as synthesized, and diagnosticScriptAndRange (converters.go:582-585) short-circuits when source!='' by returning the original text script with the range unchanged. Mapping again would interpret an original offset as a virtual offset and displace/clamp it; the Source() guard prevents that.\n\nTrace of a diagnostic span through VirtualToOriginalSpan. (a) Wholly inside copied/verbatim text: same segment, KindVerbatim, offsets interpolated, FidelityExact (spanmap_test TestVirtualToOriginalSpanVerbatim). (b) Wholly inside an atom: same segment, KindAtom/Alias, returns the atom's entire original span, FidelityAtom; interior positions are not interpolatable (TestVirtualToOriginalSpanAtom). (c) Wholly inside a gap: both endpoints lie in no segment (startIn==endIn==false), so it collapses to insertionPoint(prev) (preceding segment's OriginalEnd, or 0) as a zero-length range with FidelityNone (TestVirtualToOriginalSpanSynthesizedGap; an empty map behaves the same, TestVirtualToOriginalSpanEmptyIsSynthesized). (d) Crossing a copied segment and a gap: endpoints land in different segments/dispositions, so mapLow/mapHigh clamp the verbatim boundary and use the gap's insertion point for the other, returning FidelityApproximate. Approximate differs from 'no counterpart': approximate still yields a genuine best-effort original range bounded by mapped segment edges, while FidelityNone has no original location at all — the range is the zero-length insertion point, and diagnostic conversion replaces it with a file-level Range{} (converters.go:539-543) or the aggregate at (0,0).\n\nZero-length on a segment boundary. VirtualToOriginalSpan sends zero-length ranges to VirtualToOriginalPosition (spanmap.go:247-250, 313). On a shared boundary that is a segment's virtual start, segmentIndexAt's binary search finds that segment, so the point resolves to that segment's OriginalStart (FidelityExact for verbatim, FidelityAtom for atom). The final segment's exclusive end is deliberately treated as contained by segmentIndexAt (line 386), so a zero-length diagnostic at the last segment end still maps exactly (spanmap_test TestZeroLengthSpansAtSegmentEnds: position 30 -> 210, FidelityExact) instead of falling into a trailing gap.\n\nUTF-16 and non-BMP. positionNormalizer is built with the negotiated encoding: utf-8 uses byte length directly; utf-16 uses ast.ComputePositionMap and length = UTF8ToUTF16(len(text)) (hostimpl.go:1323-1335). normalize (line 1342) converts the mapper's index to a byte offset and rejects it if it is not utf8.RuneStart ('position %d splits a Unicode code point', lines 1356-1358). So for UTF-16 original content containing a non-BMP code point such as an emoji (2 UTF-16 units / 4 UTF-8 bytes), an external diagnostic whose Start lands on the low surrogate converts to a byte offset strictly inside the rune and the whole transform fails as an invalid response (TestRunnerRejectsPositionsInsideUnicodeCharacters uses UTF-16 with '😀' and Start 1). The same check catches a UTF-8 offset inside a 2-byte rune ('é').\n\nInvalid supplemental after a valid canonical. decodeTransformResult decodes the canonical first (hostimpl.go:1126-1135) and then iterates supplementals (1136-114